 **Parameters:**
-*   **Transfer Function Type:** 1D (data value only) or 2D (data value and gradient magnitude) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Texture Width for 1D TF:** 512 texels, mapping 65,536 16-bit values [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Early Ray Termination Threshold:** Typically 1.0 or close to it [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Bounding Cube:** Defines the ray start and end points [S1].
+*   **Transfer Function (TF):** Maps data values to colors and opacities. Supports 1D or 2D parameterization [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Texture Width:** 512 texels for 1D TFs [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Early Ray Termination Threshold:** Accumulated opacity close to 1 [S2, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Mapping 65,536 values to 512 texels in 1D TFs requires careful interpolation or quantization to avoid loss of detail [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Occlusion:** Correctly integrating depth buffer checks from opaque geometry requires consistent coordinate spaces between the volume ray casting and the opaque rendering pass [S1].
-*   **Multiple Volumes:** When processing multiple volumes, each must be sampled in turn at each point along the ray, increasing computational cost [S1].
+*   **Precision:** Mapping 65,536 16-bit data values to a 512-texel texture requires careful interpolation or quantization to avoid artifacts [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** Ray intersection with the bounding cube must be handled correctly to avoid sampling outside the volume [S1].
+*   **Occlusion:** Depth buffer usage requires synchronization between geometry and volume rendering passes [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Shaded DVR
-Enhances base DVR with local illumination models to improve volumetric perception [S2, unofficial]. It supports early ray termination and can use multi-dimensional transfer functions, though these require additional computational resources [S2, unofficial].
+### 1D Transfer Functions
+Uses a 2D texture (512 texels wide) to map 16-bit data values. Simpler and faster but less expressive than 2D TFs [S2, unofficial].
 
-### 2D Transfer Function DVR
-Uses parameterized transfer functions with rectangular widgets in the argument space (data value vs. gradient magnitude) instead of 2D color table textures [S2, unofficial]. This allows for more precise feature investigation but requires more computation [S2, unofficial].
+### 2D Transfer Functions
+Uses parameterized rectangular widgets in the argument space (data value vs. gradient magnitude). More expressive for complex features but computationally more expensive [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Early Ray Termination
-An optimization strategy where ray traversal stops if accumulated opacity is close to 1 [S2, unofficial]. This significantly improves performance without altering visible image quality, unlike MIP which must traverse the entire ray path [S2, unofficial].
+An optimization strategy that terminates ray traversal when accumulated opacity is high. Improves performance without visible image quality changes [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per pixel, where $N$ is the number of samples along the ray [G]. The programmable transfer function call adds computational overhead compared to a simple texture lookup, but reduces memory bandwidth pressure [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low memory footprint for transfer functions compared to large 2D/3D lookup tables [G]. Volume data storage depends on the dataset size [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Early ray termination significantly improves rendering performance [S2, unofficial]. The trade-off of computation for memory fetches reduces lookup time and enables swift modifications to the transfer function through parameter modification [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time parameter modification, reduced CPU-GPU data transfer, support for complex multi-dimensional transfer functions, early ray termination [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Higher computational cost per sample due to function evaluation; potential precision loss in 1D TF mapping if not handled carefully [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When interactive adjustment of transfer functions is required, or when memory bandwidth is a bottleneck. Suitable for visualizing complex scalar fields like WiFi signals or medical data [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When maximum raw performance is critical and transfer functions are static, or when the hardware has very limited ALU capability relative to memory bandwidth [G].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples along the ray. Early ray termination reduces the average number of samples per ray [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on volume data size and acceleration structure. The PTF approach reduces memory bandwidth usage by replacing texture lookups with function calls [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Early ray termination significantly improves rendering performance compared to MIP [S2, unofficial]. The trade-off is increased computation per sample due to the PTF function call [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time parameter modification, reduced CPU-GPU data transfer, support for occlusion, and improved volumetric perception with shading [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Higher computational cost per sample compared to texture-based TFs. Limited usage in medical examinations compared to MIP [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When real-time interaction with transfer function parameters is required, or when visualizing complex datasets with many interactions (e.g., WiFi signal space) [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When maximum performance is critical and real-time parameter tuning is not needed, or when simple intensity projection (MIP) is sufficient [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of Programmable Transfer Functions was introduced to address the bottleneck of updating transfer function textures on the GPU, which requires CPU-GPU data transfers [S1]. It leverages the programmability of modern graphics pipelines to compute optical properties on the fly [S1]. Shaded DVR, a related technique, has been used in medical examinations, though its usage is more limited compared to MIP [S2, unofficial]. The technique is applicable to various domains, including scientific visualization and signal space mapping [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** The concept of trading transfer function lookups for function calls was introduced to address the bottleneck of CPU-GPU data transfer when editing conventional transfer functions [S1].
+*   **History:** The technique is part of a broader family of Direct Volume Rendering methods. Shaded DVR is used in medical examinations, though less commonly than MIP [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Applied in visualizing complex scalar fields, such as WiFi signal strengths, where user interaction with isosurfaces and signal strength fall-off is important [S1].
 
